{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on d8f632fc (S1 fork readiness blocked by a head operation whose provider outcome is unknown)\n\n1. The original stays stopped. The recorded head attempt keeps its unknown outcome as a retained witness. No resend, no fabricated answer, no replacement of the lane, hold or room, and no inferred retry. The source-only recovery limit report (89922e3f) is accepted as the record of why: hold@2 admits a head answer only as the attempt's own answer, HOLD_ATTEMPTS.head is 1, and the workspace host has no recovery or replies port.\n\n2. The gap is a platform finding, not an S1 defect. File it as a child of J4 (0c3091fa), data and design only, named \"head outcome recovery for a hold\": when the provider's answer to a head operation is unknown, the hold needs either a declared recovery read bound to the original attempt (the provider ref, read once, recorded as that attempt's late answer with its ordinal) or a declared retry step with its own attempt count. Which of the two, and how the journal observer records a late outcome, is the design question. Rest the child on this ruling and on the recovery report; it goes after the J4 live witness and before J5. The builder's bounded read-only original-operation diagnostic proposal belongs to that child, with the same limits (no arbitrary private bodies, no provider probe).\n\n3. S1 continues on a fresh scratch room. The scratch room is disposable, and the blocker is a provider outcome, not S1's source. Under the already approved phased scratch runbook, start a second scratch room from the beginning with the same inputs and the gated source 52571fc2 (gate passed, 66994587), one closed phase at a time, each capture separate, the first room's evidence retained unchanged. No real room, no workspace credential beyond what the runbook already allows at each phase, no landing. If the second room's head attempt also ends unknown, stop and report; that doubles the evidence for the finding.\n\n4. The custody qualification in 89922e3f is noted: the head operation's internal READ token creation and revocation is the provider's, and the unknown outcome proves nothing about it. The finding child records that too.\n\nChild d5385b16 (seven paths) and the whole-S1 review are unchanged by this ruling.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:89922e3f90a892c5d90198657cf81597d12a5b9b, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d8f632fc2e25572c4f29b8f14ac412ae222a534c stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:070231aadcb17348891458bc2eedb5bbf529860c","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}